# LeadsNebula Rust Application Rules

## Core Principles

### Simplicity and Elegance First
- Prioritize simple, elegant solutions over complex ones
- Avoid over-engineering
- Prefer standard library and well-established patterns
- Code should be self-documenting through clear naming

### No Clutter Policy
- No temporary solutions (unless troubleshooting or debugging)
- No band-aids or workarounds
- Remove debug code, test endpoints, and unused files immediately
- Keep the codebase lean and focused

### Professional Organization
- Follow Rust conventions and best practices
- Use workspace structure for multi-crate projects
- Organize code into logical modules
- Maintain consistent naming and structure

### Documentation Policy (External Only)
- No documentation files in the repository root (except README.md)
- All documentation maintained externally (wiki, knowledge base, etc.)
- README.md should be minimal and focused on essential development information only
- Code should be self-documenting through clear naming and structure

### No Assumptions
- Never make assumptions about requirements or implementation
- Ask for clarification when requirements are ambiguous
- Verify dependencies and versions before implementing
- Test changes locally when possible before committing

### Code Quality Standards
- All code must pass `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy` checks
- Use `--locked` flag for all cargo commands in CI/CD
- Follow Rust naming conventions (snake_case for functions/variables, PascalCase for types)
- Prefer `Result<T>` over panics for error handling
- Use appropriate error types (avoid generic `anyhow` where specific errors are better)

### File Organization
- Keep related code together
- Use modules to organize functionality
- Avoid deep nesting (max 3-4 levels)
- Group imports logically (std, external, internal)

### Lean Application Philosophy
- Only include dependencies that are actively used
- Use feature flags for optional heavy dependencies
- Remove unused transitive dependencies regularly
- Optimize for build time and binary size

### No Hardcoding Policy
- Never hardcode values that should be configurable
- Use configuration files, environment variables, or computed values
- Configuration files are the single source of truth
- Avoid duplicating values across multiple places
- Prefer reading from config over hardcoding in code or workflows
- Extract values from config files dynamically rather than hardcoding

## Implementation Guidelines

### Dependencies
- Minimize dependencies
- Use feature flags for optional components (Sentry, WebAuthn, OTP)
- Audit and remove unused transitive dependencies regularly
- Prefer well-maintained crates with active communities

### Error Handling
- Use `Result<T, E>` for recoverable errors
- Use appropriate error types (avoid generic errors where specific ones are better)
- Log errors appropriately (use Sentry for production)
- Provide meaningful error messages

### Configuration
- Load configuration from SSM Parameter Store in production
- Fall back to environment variables in development
- Use batched SSM fetches to reduce latency
- Cache configuration values appropriately

### Testing
- Write tests for critical functionality
- Use integration tests for API endpoints
- Keep tests fast and focused

### CI/CD
- All workflows must use `Swatinem/rust-cache@v2` for dependency caching
- Use `--locked` flag for all cargo commands
- Build Docker images in GitHub Actions, not remotely
- Deploy pre-built images to Fly.io using `--image` flag
- Enforce environment separation at workflow level

